{
  "id": "swapping-nodes-in-a-linked-list",
  "questionId": "swapping-nodes-in-a-linked-list",
  "slug": "swapping-nodes-in-a-linked-list",
  "title": "Swapping Nodes in a Linked List",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Linked list",
  "patterns": [
    "linked list",
    "two pointers"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "finding the k-th node from the start is easy - what's awkward about finding the k-th from the end without knowing the length upfront?",
    "what if a second pointer started moving only once the first pointer was already k nodes ahead?",
    "advance a 'front' pointer k-1 steps to mark the k-th node from the start, then start a second 'end' pointer at the head and advance both together until 'front' reaches the last node",
    "when the lead pointer hits the end, the trailing pointer is exactly at the k-th node from the end - swap the two values you've marked"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "off-by-one errors converting between 1-indexed k and 0-indexed array positions",
    "computing the k-th from the end incorrectly when it's actually the same node as the k-th from the start",
    "doing two separate full passes when a single combined pass with two pointers is cleaner and avoids needing the length upfront"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "A singly linked list is given to you as an array `nums`, where `nums[i]` is the value of the i-th node in order.\n\n**Swap the value of the `k`th node from the beginning with the value of the `k`th node from the end (both 1-indexed), then return the resulting list's values in order.**\n\nThe two nodes may be the same node, in which case the swap is a no-op. Either way, only values are swapped, the list's structure and length stay the same.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= nums.length <= 10^5",
      "1 <= k <= nums.length"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "a single-node list",
      "k refers to the same node from both ends (near the middle of an odd-length list)",
      "k = 1 (the very first and very last nodes)"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5], k = 2",
        "output": "[1, 4, 3, 2, 5]",
        "expectedOutput": "[1, 4, 3, 2, 5]",
        "explanation": "the 2nd node from the start (value 2) and the 2nd node from the end (value 4) swap values"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [7, 9, 6, 6, 7, 8, 3, 0, 9, 5], k = 5",
        "output": "[7, 9, 6, 6, 8, 7, 3, 0, 9, 5]",
        "expectedOutput": "[7, 9, 6, 6, 8, 7, 3, 0, 9, 5]",
        "explanation": ""
